day","trip","job","visit"],"source":"Digi Biashara Complete 150 Business Category Template Master Blueprint (109-page PDF)","status":"published","purpose":"Agency transactions, member accounts or specialist trading","reports":["Cash/float reconciliation","transaction totals","fees/commissions","member balances","asset utilisation","ageing"],"features":["Transaction/agency or member ledger","approvals","float/asset reconciliation and audit controls"],"description":"Agency transactions, member accounts or specialist trading","default_roles":["Owner","agent/teller","supervisor","cashier","accounts/compliance. Use least-privilege permissions; sensitive actions such as refunds","stock adjustments","discounts","member payouts or record exports should require explicit permissions and audit logs"],"main_workflow":"Record authorised transaction; capture reference and amount; reconcile float/ledger; approve exceptions; issue receipt; close day","source_section":"15.10","feature_toggles":{"mpesa":false,"sales":true,"staff":true,"reports":true,"expenses":true,"customers":true,"inventory":false,"stock_movements":false},"planned_features":["Transaction/agency or member ledger","approvals","float/asset reconciliation and audit controls"],"product_examples":[{"kind":"service","name":"Consultation","unit":"hour","unit_options":["hour","session"],"editable_example":true,"initial_quantity":0,"is_template_example":true,"suggested_buying_price":null,"suggested_selling_price":null},{"kind":"service","name":"project fee","unit":"service","unit_options":["piece","service"],"editable_example":true,"initial_quantity":0,"is_template_example":true,"suggested_buying_price":null,"suggested_selling_price":null},{"kind":"service","name":"retainer","unit":"service","unit_options":["piece","service"],"editable_example":true,"initial_quantity":0,"is_template_example":true,"suggested_buying_price":null,"suggested_selling_price":null},{"kind":"service","name":"deliverable","unit":"service","unit_options":["piece","service"],"editable_example":true,"initial_quantity":0,"is_template_example":true,"suggested_buying_price":null,"suggested_selling_price":null},{"kind":"service","name":"travel","unit":"service","unit_options":["piece","service"],"editable_example":true,"initial_quantity":0,"is_template_example":true,"suggested_buying_price":null,"suggested_selling_price":null},{"kind":"service","name":"expenses","unit":"service","unit_options":["piece","service"],"editable_example":true,"initial_quantity":0,"is_template_example":true,"suggested_buying_price":null,"suggested_selling_price":null}],"setup_validation":"Create editable categories and units; leave prices blank until the business owner sets them; record opening stock/balances only when supplied; require a branch/location only if the business uses branches; retain created_by, business_id and timestamps; prevent duplicate codes within the same business. Separate principal/customer funds from business income and commission; require transaction references and dual approval for configured high-risk adjustments. <PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 108 / 109> Platform implementation specification The category-template system should be added to the existing Digi Biashara application after codebase and schema inspection. The names below are logical entities; reuse existing tables and naming conventions where possible rather than creating duplicate systems. A. Logical data model Entity Purpose / key data business_groups Group id, name, description, display order, active status. business_categories Category id, group id, name, description, icon, active status, display order. category_templates Template id, category id, version, status (draft/published/archived), schema/config JSON, created_by, published_at. template_catalogue_items Template item/service name, item type, unit, optional variant/field defaults, default price null, sort order. template_features Feature key, enabled/disabled/planned state, dependency, configuration and release status. business_template_assignments Business id, category id, copied template version, applied_at, applied_by, migration/source metadata. business_custom_fields Tenant-owned field definitions and values; validate field type and access classification. business_products_services Tenant-owned catalogue records copied from template and subsequently edited by that business. B. Registration flow ● Admin selects Add Business and enters core business details. ● Admin searches or browses group → category; category selection loads a read-only preview of the currently published template. ● Preview displays modules, starter catalogue, units, fields and any planned/disabled capabilities. ● Admin confirms; server validates permission and creates the tenant and owner membership. ● Within one database transaction, copy template configuration and starter catalogue into tenant-owned records; prices remain null unless explicitly set by admin. ● Create the template assignment/version record and audit event; return the new business workspace. ● If any step fails, roll back all created tenant/template records; retry must be idempotent. C. Admin template management ● List 15 groups and 150 categories with search, filters, active status and counts. ● Open a category to edit template identity, feature flags, starter items/services, units, custom fields, workflows/help text, reports and suggested roles. ● Support draft → preview → publish, version history, duplicate template, archive and restore. ● Validate required fields, unique category mapping, safe field types and dependencies before publishing. ● Show which businesses were created from each version; never auto-overwrite their live setup. D. Feature readiness states Use explicit states such as available, planned, disabled and requires configuration. A template must not advertise an unavailable feature as operational. For example, appointments, fleet trips, production batches, school attendance, room bookings, payroll/commissions and clinical records need their own complete user flows, data model, permissions and reports before they can be switched on. E. Security, tenant isolation and audit ● Review every table touched by the new feature for RLS and tenant-scoped foreign keys. ● Use authenticated server-side checks for admin-only template edits and publishing. <PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 109 / 109> ● Never trust a client-supplied business_id without verifying the user''s membership and permission. ● Use audit events for template publish, template application, price changes, stock adjustments, refunds, payment reversals, permission changes and data exports. ● Use separate permission groups for sensitive records; do not make all staff users administrators. ● For cross-tenant platform administration, use a narrowly scoped server-side role and log every access. F. Testing and release gates ● Seed test: exactly 15 groups and 150 active categories, with no duplicate names/IDs and stable ordering. ● Template test: each of 150 categories resolves to one published template version and expected category-specific starter content. ● Registration test: create sample businesses from every group; verify correct catalogue, fields, roles and feature states. ● Isolation test: attempt cross-tenant reads/writes through UI, API and direct database requests; all must be denied. ● Regression test: existing sales, stock, invoices, expenses, branches, customers, suppliers, reports and M-Pesa flows remain functional. ● Template update test: publish a new version and confirm existing businesses remain unchanged unless they explicitly accept a migration. ● Failure/retry test: simulate partial failure and repeated registration; verify rollback and no duplicates. ● Mobile/responsive and accessibility test; staging sign-off; production backup and rollback plan. G. Recommended delivery sequence ● Stage 1 — Inspect: map current code, database schema, authentication, business registration and existing modules. Prepare a change plan before migrations. ● Stage 2 — Foundation: create/extend groups, categories, template versions and admin management screens. ● Stage 3 — Template data: seed all 150 category templates from this blueprint, including starter catalogue and category fields. ● Stage 4 — Registration: integrate category selection, preview and safe tenant-owned template copying. ● Stage 5 — Specialised modules: prioritise based on customer demand; build complete workflows before enabling feature flags. ● Stage 6 — QA and deployment: run security/regression tests on staging, review migrations and deploy with monitoring. H. Important product decisions ● Default currency: KES; allow configuration if the business operates elsewhere. ● Tax: configurable by business and transaction type; do not hard-code one tax treatment across all categories. ● Prices: blank by default; each business sets its own price list. ● Branches: support the existing maximum of five branches; do not assume every category needs multiple branches. ● Starter items: mark as sample records and allow delete/edit; do not treat them as opening stock. ● Data ownership: each business owns its transactions, customers, suppliers, catalogue and customisations. ● Template lifecycle: master template changes affect new registrations by default; existing tenants opt into reviewed upgrades. Final implementation instruction Treat this blueprint as the source specification for the 150 category templates. Before writing code, inspect the current Digi Biashara repository and Supabase schema. Preserve existing working features and data. Do not reset, drop or rewrite production tables. Propose migrations, explain any new specialised module, implement in small reviewed stages, test tenant isolation and existing workflows, and deploy only after staging verification. End of document — 15 groups, 150 categories","template_version":2,"privacy_sensitive":true,"supported_modules":["sales","expenses","customers","staff","reports"],"configuration